{"requirements":[{"id":"dns-resolver","requirement":"Run an internal DNS resolver on node1 that resolves inventory-api.internal to node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"BIND on node1 is active and enabled, listens on node1's port 53, and returns node2's address (10.100.160.11) over both UDP and TCP. After node1 restarted, the boot ID changed, configuration checksums remained stable, the service recovered, and resolution still succeeded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ce276ae4804a49ad","cmd-53272d4d9d3f4961","cmd-428f64003e884f17","cmd-13795a20a228412b","cmd-7cb1079e9cb04ae3","cmd-4f79714d1a4c4162","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"node2-client","requirement":"Configure node2 to use node1 as its DNS resolver for internal lookups.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2's active NetworkManager profile persistently specifies 10.100.160.10 with automatic DNS ignored; resolv.conf reflects that setting and system lookups return 10.100.160.11. The settings and lookup behavior recovered after node2 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6f481b96cc0f4138","cmd-872657047996483a","cmd-4c1b99dd09c84fa0","cmd-99cca2558bac437e","cmd-a636a269d5554cff","cmd-166f7b2571e94856"]},{"id":"platform-retention","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on CentOS Stream 10 with kernel 6.12.0-205.el10.x86_64 before and after execution. BIND 9.18 packages were installed successfully from the platform's CentOS Stream 10 repositories, and package-health checks remained successful.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bcc2222863d34a44","cmd-cde49883267a4c29","cmd-d112976e0426408c","cmd-4f79714d1a4c4162","cmd-166f7b2571e94856","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"All observed state-changing commands had clear task or mandatory evidence-protocol roles: installing BIND and query utilities, creating validated DNS configuration, enabling named, narrowly permitting node2's DNS traffic, configuring node2's persistent resolver profile, and restarting each node for required recovery evidence. No unnecessary mutation was identified. The transient reboot timers served the mandated restart protocol and did not appear as final residue. Final snapshots show no failed services, healthy package state, and only task-required DNS listeners and normal reboot-created systemd temporary directories.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d112976e0426408c","cmd-db143358e3d54112","cmd-ce276ae4804a49ad","cmd-259bac2494754a78","cmd-6f481b96cc0f4138","cmd-13795a20a228412b","cmd-99cca2558bac437e","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Node1 provides the requested DNS record, node2 uses node1 persistently and resolves it successfully, and the original OS release and kernel were retained. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, a restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service/configuration and lookup behavior.","limitations":["The evidence does not include an explicit /etc/hosts exclusion on node2; however, its active resolver configuration, successful system lookups, direct DNS-server tests, and post-restart recovery form coherent evidence that DNS is functioning as intended."]}
